                                                               Maximum spectral sum of graphs
                                            Hitesh Kumar, Lele Liu, Hermie Monterde, Shivaramakrishna Pragada, Michael Tait


                                                                                            Abstract
arXiv:2604.00512v2 [math.CO] 3 May 2026




                                                    For a graph G of order n, the spectral sum of G is defined to be the sum λ1 (G) + λ2 (G), where
                                                λ1 (G) (resp. λ2 (G)) is the largest (resp. second largest) adjacency eigenvalue of G. Ebrahimi,
                                                Mohar, Nikiforov and Ahmady (2008) conjectured that the spectral sum
                                                                                                           8
                                                                                      λ1 (G) + λ2 (G) ≤      n
                                                                                                           7
                                                for any graph G. We prove this conjecture by combining tools from the theory of graph limits,
                                                convex geometry, exterior algebra and convex optimization. The techniques developed are of
                                                independent interest.

                                          Keywords: Spectral sum, Second eigenvalue, Graphon, Convexity, Exterior algebra, Matrix Sum
                                          of Squares
                                          MSC2020: 05C50, 15A18, 15A60, 52A38, 15A75, 15A39

                                          1. Introduction
                                              Consider a simple undirected graph G = (V, E) with vertex set V = {1, 2, . . . , n}. The adjacency
                                          matrix of G is defined to be the square matrix A(G) = [aij ] of order n, where aij = 1 if i and j are
                                          adjacent, and aij = 0 otherwise. We order the eigenvalues of A(G) as λ1 (G) ≥ λ2 (G) ≥ · · · ≥ λn (G).
                                          The first eigenvalue λ1 (G) is called the spectral radius of G. The sum of the first two largest
                                          eigenvalues λ1 (G) + λ2 (G) is called the spectral sum of G. For n ∈ N, let G(n) denote the set of
                                          simple undirected graphs on n vertices. We denote the complement of G by G.
                                              Extremization of graph eigenvalues, particularly λ1 , λ2 and λn , over various graph families
                                          is an important and well-studied problem in spectral graph theory, see the surveys [9, 10, 28].
                                          Investigation of combinations of eigenvalues, such as Nordhaus-Gaddum type sums λi (G) + λi (G)
                                          [2, 24, 29], λ1 − λ2 (spectral gap) [27, 28], λ1 − λn (spectral spread ) [6], and λ1 + λn (a measure of
                                          bipartiteness) [8], has also led to interesting results, techniques and applications.
                                              Recall that the energy E(G) of a graph G on n vertices is defined as the sum of the absolute
                                          values of eigenvalues of G. Equivalently,
                                                                                                     k
                                                                                                     X
                                                                                  E(G) = 2 max             λi (G).
                                                                                             1≤k≤n
                                                                                                     i=1

                                          Thus, the sum of the k largest eigenvalues of a graph sheds light on the energy of the graph. Mohar
                                          [21] established an upper bound on ki=1 λi (G) for 1 ≤ k ≤ n and provided further details on its
                                                                               P


                                                                                                1
role in theoretical chemistry. Mohar’s bound was improved further by Das, Mojallal and Sun [11].
Gernert1 investigated the case k = 2 and proved that the inequality λ1 (G) + λ2 (G) ≤ n holds for
various graph families, including regular graphs, triangle-free graphs and planar graphs. Gernert
asked whether this inequality holds for any graph. Nikiforov [23] answered this question in the
                                                                                        √
negative and also proved that the spectral sum of graphs in G(n) is upper bounded by 2n/ 3. Later,
Ebrahimi, Mohar, Nikiforov, and Ahmady [12] presented an improved upper bound on the spectral
sum as described below.

Theorem 1.1 ([12]). For any graph G ∈ G(n), we have
                                                            8.0185
                                        λ1 (G) + λ2 (G) <          n.
                                                               7
   Moreover, in [12] the authors constructed an infinite family of graphs of order n = 7k which
have spectral sum 8k − 2 = 87 n − 2 and suggested that 8n/7 is the correct upper bound for the
spectral sum. In this paper, we prove their conjecture.

Theorem 1.2. For any G ∈ G(n), we have
                                                           8
                                          λ1 (G) + λ2 (G) ≤ n.
                                                           7
    We prove Theorem 1.2 by translating the problem to graphons and then utilizing analytic tools
to show that the spectral sum of graphons is at most 8/7 (Theorem 3.5). Our proof is inspired by
Terpai’s [29] (cf. [15]) maximization proof of the sum λ1 (G) + λ1 (G), and Breen, Riasanovsky, Tait
and Urschel’s [6] maximization proof of the spread λ1 − λn using graphons. However, there is a
significant difficulty when considering the spectral sum that we must overcome here which requires
novel methods. When considering either λ1 (G) + λ1 (G) or λ1 − λn , both of these maximization
problems can be expressed as the maximization of a two-term sum of quadratic forms over pairs of
unit vectors (or L 2 -functions when working with graphons). Because of this, one can do alterations
to the purported extremal graph (or graphon) and its eigenvectors and estimate from below the
objective function by considering Rayleigh quotients. The major difficulty with the spectral sum is
that in this case, one has to maximize a two-term sum of quadratic forms over pairs of orthogonal
vectors. The orthogonality condition usually breaks down when one tries to modify the eigenvector(s)
on a subset of vertices, and so any modification needs to be chosen carefully. We introduce some new
ideas to overcome this, and we believe that the techniques used in this paper may find applications
in other problems where one wishes to optimize a sum of quadratic forms over orthogonal vectors.
High-level outline of our proof of Theorem 1.2. We first show that it suffices to prove the
weaker bound 87 n + o(n) for the spectral sum of graphs (Theorem 2.2) in Section 2. We then
give a brief introduction to graphons and relate the spectral sum of graphs with that of graphons
(Lemma 3.4) in Section 3. Thus, it suffices to show that the spectral sum of graphons is at most 8/7
(Theorem 3.5). We prove the result for graphons in Section 4. We begin with an extremal graphon
W (x, y) which has maximum spectral sum and maximum value of its largest eigenvalue among all
  1
      as described by Nikiforov [23].


                                                    2
such graphons. We establish the adjacency criteria for W (x, y) (Lemma 4.1) which describes the
points (x, y) ∈ [0, 1]2 where W (x, y) = 1. Assuming µ1 and f (resp. µ2 and g) denote the largest
(resp. the second largest) eigenvalue and the corresponding eigenfunction of W , we show that f and
g satisfy an ellipse equation for a.e. x ∈ [0, 1] (Lemma 4.2). Using Carathéodory’s Theorem from
convex geometry, we then prove that f and g can be assumed to be step-functions with at most 6
steps a.e. (Lemma 4.6). We reduce the problem to analyzing the spectral sum of weighted graph(s)
G∗ of order at most 6 (Lemma 4.8). Using structural arguments, we determine the possibilities
for G∗ in subsection 4.6 and show that it suffices to upper bound the spectral sum of the unique
weighted graph H6 shown in Figure 1 (Lemma 4.11). We prove Lemma 4.11 by first reducing it to a
positive semi-definiteness verifiability problem (Lemma 4.14) using exterior algebra, and then using
the matrix sum of squares technique from convex optimization to do the verification.


2. Asymptotic result suffices
    We observe the following using a graph-blowup argument originally by Nikiforov (cf. [7]).

Lemma 2.1. Suppose for any graph G ∈ G(n), we have
                                                          8n
                                      λ1 (G) + λ2 (G) ≤      + o(n).
                                                           7
Then, Theorem 1.2 holds for any G ∈ G(n).

Proof. For a graph G ∈ G(n), consider G(t) , the graph obtained by replacing every vertex of G by
an empty graph of order t, where the copies of two vertices of G are adjacent in G(t) if and only if
the two vertices are adjacent in G. The graph G(t) is called the (open)-t-blowup of G. It is known
(see [22]) that the eigenvalues of G(t) are precisely tλi (G) (i = 1, . . . , n) and n(t − 1) additional 0’s.
By assumption, we have
                                                            8tn
                                  λ1 (G(t) ) + λ2 (G(t) ) ≤     + o(tn).
                                                             7
Since λ1 (G(t) ) = tλ1 (G) and λ2 (G(t) ) = tλ2 (G), it follows that
                                                          8tn
                                    tλ1 (G) + tλ2 (G) ≤       + o(tn).
                                                           7
Dividing by t and considering the limit as t → ∞, we get
                                                              8n
                                          λ1 (G) + λ2 (G) ≤      ,
                                                               7
completing the proof of the lemma.

    Thus, in order to prove Theorem 1.2, it is sufficient to prove the following asymptotic result.

Theorem 2.2. For any graph G ∈ G(n), we have
                                                          8n
                                      λ1 (G) + λ2 (G) ≤      + o(n).
                                                           7

