3. Enter graphons
     Graphons are analytic objects that generalize graphs, originally defined in an influential paper
by Lovász and Szegedy [17] and the monograph by Lovász [16] (cf. [4]). We first give the necessary
background on graphons, which is needed for further development in this paper.
     A graphon is a symmetric Lebesgue measurable function K : [0, 1]2 → [0, 1]. We denote the set
of graphons by K. A graphon K ∈ K is called step-graphon if there exists a partition U1 , . . . , Uk of
[0, 1] such that K is constant on Ui × Uj for all i, j. If U ⊆ [0, 1]2 is a symmetric (i.e., (x, y) ∈ U if
and only if (y, x) ∈ U ) measurable set, then its indicator function χU , defined by
                                                  (
                                                   1,           if (x, y) ∈ U ;
                                    χU (x, y) =
                                                       0,       otherwise,

is clearly a step-graphon. A graphon generalizes the notion of a graph as follows: given a (unweighted)
graph G ∈ G(n) with vertex set V (G) = {1, . . . , n}, one can naturally define a subset
                                [ u − 1 u v − 1 v 
                                            ,     ×         ,    ⊆ [0, 1]2 ,
                                u∼v     n     n         n     n
                              u,v∈V (G)

which is symmetric and measurable, whose indicator function (which we denote by χG for brevity)
is a step-graphon. Clearly, for a given graph G, many graphons can be associated corresponding to
different vertex labellings of G. Hence, an equivalence relation is defined on the set of graphons K
using the cut norm defined below so that for any graph G there is a unique (up to isomorphism)
equivalence class of graphons corresponding to G.
    For K ∈ K, its cut norm ∥K∥□ is defined as
                                                 Z
                                ∥K∥□ = sup            K(x, y) dx dy .
                                          S,T ⊆[0,1]       S×T

Let δ□ be the cut semidistance on K given by

            δ□ (U, W ) = inf{∥U − W φ ∥□ : φ is a measure-preserving bijection on [0, 1]},

where U, W ∈ K, and W φ (x, y) = W (φ(x), φ(y)). Define a relation ∼ on K as follows: U ∼ W if
and only if δ□ (U, W ) = 0. It is known that ∼ is an equivalence relation and the quotient space
K/ ∼ is a compact metric space, see [18, Theorem 5.1].
    For a measurable subset U ⊆ [0, 1]2 , we will denote its Lebesgue measure by m(U ). We say that
two measurable subsets of [0, 1]2 are equal almost everywhere (a.e. for short) if their symmetric
difference has measure zero. Consider the Hilbert space L 2 [0, 1] of square-integrable functions
