    Recall the well-known Min-Max Theorem that relates eigenvalues to quadratic forms.

Theorem 3.1 (Min-Max Theorem). Let A be a compact Hermitian operator on a Hilbert space H
with inner product ⟨ · , · ⟩. Let U denote a subspace of H. Then the k-th largest eigenvalue λk of A
is given by
                                      λk = max       min ⟨z, Az⟩.
                                                          U      z∈U
                                                      dim(U )=k ∥z∥=1

Moreover, if z1 , . . . , zk ∈ H are mutually orthonormal vectors such that λi = ⟨zi , Azi ⟩ for i = 1, . . . , k,
then zi is a λi -eigenvector for A.

   Let µ1 (K) and µ2 (K) denote the largest and the second largest eigenvalue of AK . Then, by
Theorem 3.1, we have

                     µ1 (K) =     max          f Kf     and    µ2 (K) =        max       min gKg.           (3.1)
                                f ∈L 2 [0,1]                                U ⊂L 2 [0,1] g∈U
                                  ∥f ∥2 =1                                  dim(U )=2 ∥g∥2 =1

Furthermore, if f ∈ L 2 [0, 1] is a unit function such that µ1 (K) = f Kf , then f is a µ1 (K)-
eigenfunction of K (up to a set of measure zero). Similarly, if f is a µ1 (K)-eigenfunction and
g ∈ L 2 [0, 1] is a unit function orthogonal to f such that µ2 (K) = gKg, then g is a µ2 (K)-
eigenfunction of K. Also, one can always choose a µ1 (K)-eigenfunction f which is non-negative.
    We define σ(K) := µ1 (K) + µ2 (K) and we call it the spectral sum of the graphon K. We note
the following easy consequence of Theorem 3.1, which we will use repeatedly.

Lemma 3.2. For K ∈ K/ ∼, we have

                           σ(K) = µ1 (K) + µ2 (K) =                   max          f Kf + gKg.
