In particular, there exists a graphon (which we call extremal graphon) that achieves the maximum.
    As expected, the spectral sum of a graph G is related to the spectral sum of its corresponding
graphon χG as described in the following lemma. It can be proved by replacing the eigenvectors of
G with their corresponding step-functions, see [15] or [29] for details.
Lemma 3.4. For a graph G of order n, we have

                           λ1 (G) = nµ1 (χG )      and    λ2 (G) = nµ2 (χG ).

   In view of Lemma 3.4, the following result implies Theorem 2.2.
Theorem 3.5. For any graphon K ∈ K/ ∼, we have σ(K) ≤ 8/7.
   We prove Theorem 3.5 in the following section.

4. Proof of Theorem 3.5
     Throughout this section, W ∈ K/ ∼ will denote a graphon that maximizes the spectral sum
σ( · ) over K/ ∼ and has the maximum value of its largest eigenvalue among all such graphons.
     We will denote by µ1 and f (resp. µ2 and g) the largest (resp. the second largest) eigenvalue
and the corresponding eigenfunction of W . This means
