Since µ1 ≤ 1, we conclude that µ2 ≥ 1/7. In particular, this means that µ2 is always positive. We
will often use this technical detail without mention in later proofs.

4.1. Adjacency criterion
    Since f is the eigenfunction for µ1 , we can assume that f is non-negative. Define the positive
support of g to be the set S + (g) = {x ∈ [0, 1] : g(x) > 0}. The negative support S − (g) and the
zero set S 0 (g) are defined analogously. The support of g is given by S(g) = S + (g) ∪ S − (g). For
convenience, for any (x, y) ∈ [0, 1]2 , we define

                                   κ(x, y) := f (x)f (y) + g(x)g(y).

We first observe the following eigen-entry condition for an element (x, y) ∈ [0, 1]2 to be contained in
the support of the extremal graphon W . When translated to graphs, this condition tells us when
two vertices are adjacent in the extremal graph, and hence we call it the adjacency criterion.

