   Next, we recall two results from the theory of convexity.

Lemma 4.4 ([5, Section 2.1.4]). Let C ⊆ Rd be a convex set and x be a random vector such that
x ∈ C with probability one. Then E(x) ∈ C.

   For a subset S ⊆ Rd , the convex hull of S, denoted by conv(S), is defined to be the smallest
convex set that contains it. We recall the following well-known theorem by Carathéodory on the
convex hull of sets.

Theorem 4.5 (Carathéodory’s Theorem [20, Theorem 1.2.3]). Let S ⊆ Rd . If x ∈ conv(S), then
there exist points s0 , s1 , . . . , sd ∈ S and coefficients a0 , a1 , . . . , ad ≥ 0 with di=0 ai = 1 such that
                                                                                          P

                                                      d
                                                      X
                                                x=          ai si .
                                                      i=0

Equivalently, every point in conv(S) is a convex combination of at most d + 1 points of S.

