4.4. Monotonicity of f and g
    Without loss of generality, we can assume that g is monotonically decreasing on [0, 1]. By the
assumption on g, we see that f is monotonically increasing on E + , constant on E 0 and monotonically
decreasing on E − . In light of Lemma 4.1, it is clear that W (x, y) = 1 whenever x, y ∈ E + ∪ E 0 or
x, y ∈ E − ∪ E 0 . The value of W (x, y) is undetermined at this stage whenever x ∈ E + and y ∈ E − .
We do have the following monotonicity property.
Lemma 4.7 (Monotonicity of neighbourhoods). The following statements hold.
  (i) Let x1 , x2 ∈ E + and y ∈ E − such that x2 ≥ x1 . If W (x1 , y) = 1, then W (x2 , y) = 1.

 (ii) Let x1 , x2 ∈ E − and y ∈ E + such that x2 ≤ x1 . If W (x2 , y) = 1, then W (x1 , y) = 1.
Proof. Since x2 ≥ x1 ∈ E + , we have f (x2 ) ≥ f (x1 ) and g(x2 ) ≤ g(x1 ). Since g(y) < 0, we see that

                            f (x2 )f (y) + g(x2 )g(y) ≥ f (x1 )f (y) + g(x1 )g(y).

Assertion (i) now follows from Lemma 4.1 and the assumption that W (x1 , y) = 1. A similar
argument works for assertion (ii).

