implying αi = αj , a contradiction.

   Let us now analyze the structure of G∗ for different values of k.

Claim 4.3. If k ≤ 3, then G∗ ∼
                             = P3 .

Proof. The case k = 1 is not possible. Indeed, 1 = ∥f ∥2 = α1 and 1 = ∥g∥2 = |β1 |. This gives
0 = ⟨f, g⟩ = α1 β1 ̸= 0, a contradiction.
   If k = 2, then σ(M ∗ ) = tr(M ∗ ) = 2i=1 ui = 1 for all choices of Du . By (4.3), we know that
                                          P

σ(G∗ ) ≥ 8/7. Thus, there is no graph G∗ of order 2.

                                                 16
    Assume now that k = 3. Without loss of generality, we can assume that β1 > β2 ≥ 0 > β3 ,
since g and −g are both eigenfunctions for µ2 . Then, by Lemma 4.1, we see that U1 ∼ U2 in G∗ . It
is easy to check that the only graph on 3 vertices with at least one edge and true-twin-free is P3
(obviously with a loop on each vertex).

    We already know that Ui ∼ Ui for all 1 ≤ i ≤ k, i.e., each vertex of G∗ has loops. In the proofs
of the claims below, we will repeatedly use the fact that G∗ is true-twin free (Lemma 4.10) and
monotonicity of neighbourhoods (Lemma 4.7) without mention.

Claim 4.4. Suppose k = 4, then G∗ ∼
                                  = P4 .
Proof. In light of Lemma 4.6, one of the following occurs:
   Case 1: β1 > β2 > β3 ≥ 0 > β4 .
   Then, by Lemma 4.1, U1 , U2 , U3 induce a triangle in G∗ . If U4 ∼ U2 , then U3 ∼ U4 , implying U2
and U3 are true-twins, a contradiction. If U4 ≁ U2 , then U4 ≁ U1 , and so U1 and U2 are true-twins,
again a contradiction.
   Case 2: β1 > β2 > 0 > β3 > β4 .
   We have U1 ∼ U2 and U3 ∼ U4 . If U1 ∼ U4 , then U1 , U2 are adjacent to both U3 , U4 , implying
U1 and U2 are true-twins, a contradiction. So suppose U1 ≁ U4 .
   If U1 ∼ U3 , then U2 ∼ U3 . If U2 ≁ U4 , then again U1 and U2 are true-twins, a contradiction.
Thus, U2 ∼ U4 . But then U2 and U3 are true-twins, a contradiction. So U1 ≁ U3 , U4 . Arguing
similarly with U4 , we see that U4 ≁ U1 , U2 .
   Now, if U2 ≁ U3 , then U1 and U2 are true-twins, a contradiction. Thus, the only possibility is
U2 ∼ U3 . This completely determines G∗ , and we have G∗ ∼   = P4 .

Claim 4.5. If k = 5, then G∗ ∼
                             = H5 .
Proof. Using Lemma 4.6, without loss of generality, we can assume that

                                     β1 > β2 > β3 ≥ 0 > β4 > β5 .

Thus, U1 , U2 , U3 induce a triangle in G∗ . Moreover, U4 ∼ U5 . We consider the following cases:
    Case 1: U1 ∼ U5 .
    Then U1 is adjacent to all vertices in G∗ . This means U2 is also adjacent to all vertices, implying
U1 and U2 are true-twins, a contradiction.
    Case 2: U1 ≁ U5 and U1 ∼ U4 .
    Then U1 , U2 , U3 are all adjacent to U4 . If U2 ≁ U5 , then U1 and U2 are true-twins, a contradiction.
So suppose U2 ∼ U5 . This implies U2 and U3 are adjacent to all vertices in G∗ and hence they are
true-twins, a contradiction.
    Case 3: U1 ≁ U4 , U5 .
    If U2 ∼ U5 , then U2 and U3 are adjacent to all vertices, and hence they are true-twins, a
contradiction. So suppose U2 ≁ U5 . If U2 ≁ U4 , then U1 and U2 are true-twins, a contradiction. So
assume that U2 ∼ U4 . We have N [U2 ] = {U1 , U2 , U3 , U4 }. If U3 ≁ U5 , then U2 and U3 are true-twins,
a contradiction. So we have U3 ∼ U5 , i.e., N [U3 ] = {U1 , U2 , U3 , U4 , U5 }. This completely determines
G∗ and we see that G∗ ∼   = H5 .

                                                    17
Claim 4.6. If k = 6, then G∗ ∼
                             = H6 .
Proof. By Lemma 4.6, we have

                                   β1 > β2 > β3 > 0 > β4 > β5 > β6 .

By Lemma 4.1 we see that U1 , U2 , U3 (resp. U4 , U5 , U6 ) induce a triangle in G∗ . We consider the
following cases:
    Case 1: U1 ∼ U6 .
    Then N [U1 ] = {U1 , . . . , U6 }. This implies U2 , U3 are also adjacent to all vertices in G∗ . But
then U1 and U2 are true-twins, a contradiction.
    Case 2: U1 ≁ U6 and U1 ∼ U5 .
    Then U1 ∼ U4 and thus U2 , U3 are also adjacent to U4 , U5 . If U2 ≁ U6 , then U1 and U2 are
true-twins. If U2 ∼ U6 , then U3 ∼ U6 and so U2 and U3 are true-twins. In either case, we have a
contradiction.
    Case 3: U1 ≁ U5 , U6 and U1 ∼ U4 .
    Then U2 , U3 are adjacent to U4 .
    Subcase 3.1: U2 ∼ U6 . Then U2 and U3 are adjacent to all vertices in G∗ , a contradiction.
    Subcase 3.2: U2 ≁ U6 and U2 ∼ U5 . Then U2 , U3 are adjacent to U4 , U5 . If U3 ≁ U6 , then
U2 and U3 are true-twins. If U3 ∼ U6 , then U3 and U4 are true-twins. In both cases, we have a
contradiction.
    Subcase 3.3: U2 ≁ U5 , U6 and U2 ∼ U4 . Then U1 and U2 are true-twins, a contradiction.
    Case 4: U1 ≁ U4 , U5 , U6 .
    Arguing as above, one can show that U6 also is not adjacent to U1 , U2 , U3 . So we only need to
worry about the edges (or non-edges) between U2 , U3 and U4 , U5 . If U2 ∼ U5 , then U2 , U3 are adjacent
to U4 , U5 and so U2 , U3 are true-twins, a contradiction. So assume U2 ≁ U5 . If U2 ≁ U4 , then U1
and U2 are true-twins, a contradiction. So we have N [U2 ] = {U1 , U2 , U3 , U4 }. If U3 ≁ U5 , then U2
and U3 are true-twins, a contradiction. So the only possibility is N [U3 ] = {U1 , U2 , U3 , U4 , U5 }. This
determines the graph G∗ . Indeed, G∗ ∼     = H6 .

     The above claims complete the proof of Lemma 4.9. Interestingly, the graphs P3 , P4 , H5 are
induced subgraphs of H6 . This means that if k < 6, we can add sub-intervals with measure zero to
the list U1 , U2 , . . . , Uk so that we have a partition of [0, 1] into 6 sub-intervals. Moreover, we can
define f and g on these additional measure zero sets so that g always has non-negative values on
U1 , U2 , U3 and negative values on U4 , U5 , U6 a.e. In other words, g ≡ βi on Ui a.e. where

                                   β1 > β2 > β3 ≥ 0 > β4 > β5 > β6 .

Thus, the problem is now reduced to proving the following.
                                                         P
Lemma 4.11. For 1 ≤ i ≤ 6, let 0 ≤ ui ≤ 1 be such that i ui = 1. Let Du denote the diagonal
matrix with diagonal entries u1 , . . . , u6 . Let

                                         M ∗ = Du1/2 A(H6 )Du1/2 .

Then σ(M ∗ ) ≤ 8/7.

                                                    18
   We will prove the above Lemma 4.11 using matrix sum of squares technique from convex
optimization. Lemma 4.11 is a non-convex optimization problem which we first convert to a higher
dimensional convex optimization problem using exterior algebra.

4.7. Exterior algebra
   Let M be a real square matrix of order n. For matrices M, N , let M ⊗ N denote the usual
Kronecker product of matrices. Define

                                      N := M ⊗ In + In ⊗ M.

A combinatorial interpretation of N is that if M is the adjacency matrix of a graph, then N is the
adjacency matrix of the cartesian product of the graph with itself.
   For two vectors u, v ∈ Rn , the wedge product of u and v is given by

                                      u ∧ v := u ⊗ v − v ⊗ u.

Let e1 , . . . , en denote the standard basis of Rn . Consider the 2-nd exterior power of Rn given by

                              Λ2 (Rn ) = span{ei ∧ ej : 1 ≤ i < j ≤ n},

which is an antisymmetric subspace of the tensor space Rn ⊗ Rn . An orthonormal basis of Λ2 (Rn )
is given by                                                   
