5. Concluding remarks
    In this paper, we established a tight upper bound for the spectral sum of graphs in G(n). The
problem of characterizing graphs which attain the bound remains open (see Conjecture 5.1 below).
    For n ≥ p ≥ q ≥ 0, define K(n, p, q) to be the graph obtained by taking the complement of the
union of the complete bipartite graph Kp,q and n − p − q isolated vertices. In other words, K(n, p, q)
is the join of Kn−p−q with Kp ∪ Kq . In their survey of automated conjectures in spectral graph
theory, Aouchiche and Hansen [1] generalized the tight construction for spectral sum from [12] and
framed the following conjecture.

Conjecture 5.1 ([1, 12]). Let n ≥ 5. Then for all graphs G ∈ G(n), we have

                         λ1 (G) + λ2 (G) ≤ λ1 (K(n, p, q)) + λ2 (K(n, p, q)),

where if n = 7k + r with 0 ≤ r ≤ 6, then
                                      
                                      
                                       (2k, 2k)                 if r = 0, 1;
                                      
                                      
                                      (2k + 1, 2k)              if r = 2;
                                      
                                      
                                      
                              (p, q) =    (2k + 1, 2k + 1)       if r = 3, 4;
                                         
                                         
                                         
                                         
                                         
                                         (2k + 2, 2k + 1)       if r = 5;
                                         
                                          (2k + 2, 2k + 2)       if r = 6.
                                         

Moreover, equality holds if and only if G ∼
                                          = K(n, p, q).

    The key reason our proof of Theorem 1.2 does not fully characterize the extremal graphs
(equivalently, extremal graphons) is because of the extra assumption on W that it has the maximum
largest eigenvalue µ1 among all graphons that maximize the spectral sum. We require this extra
assumption on W at two places: first, in Lemma 4.1 to decide the value of W (x, y) when κ(x, y) = 0;
secondly, in Lemma 4.6 so that we can argue that f˜ is an eigenfunction for W     f corresponding
to eigenvalue µ1 (W                                             f in place of W . We believe that
                   f ) = µ1 , which finally allows us to choose W
bypassing this assumption on W should lead to a characterization of the extremal graphon.
    The problem of maximizing/minimizing the spectral sum for trees was recently resolved by Kumar,
Mohar, Pragada and Zhan [14]. Indeed, they considered the convex combination αλ1 + (1 − α)λ2

                                                     21
(α ∈ [0, 1]) of λ1 and λ2 of trees of order n and fully characterized the extremal trees that maximize
the convex combination for large n. It would be of interest to consider the convex combination of λ1
and λ2 for graphs in G(n) and find the optimal upper bound. One may also try to investigate the
spectral sum of other families; the family of Kr -free graphs is of some interest. The minimization of
spectral sum of graphs remains an open problem. We propose the following conjecture for interested
readers.

Conjecture 5.2. For sufficiently large n, the path uniquely minimizes the spectral sum among all
connected graphs of order n.


